Twist and Sliding Dynamics between Interpenetrated Frames in TiMOF Revealing High Proton Conductivity
DOI: 10.1039/x9xx00000x
We report the design and synthesis of titanium catecholate framework, MOF-217, comprised of 2,4,6-tri(3,4-dihydroxyphenyl)-1,3,5-triazine (TDHT) and isolated \(\mathsf{T i O}_{6}\) clusters, with 2-fold interpenetrated srs topology. The dynamics of the organic linker, breaking of \(C_{3\mathrm{h}}\) symmetry, allowed for reversible twist and sliding between interpenetrated frames upon temperature change and the inclusion of small molecules. Introduction of \(28~\mathsf{w t}\%\) imidazole into the pores of MOF-217, \(28\%\mathsf{I m}\) -in-MOF-217, resulted in four orders of magnitude increase in proton conductivity, due to the appropriate accommodation of imidazole molecules and their vehicle transfer facilitated with H-bond to MOF structure across the pores. This MOF-based proton conductor can be operated at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a proton conductivity of \(1.1\times10^{-3}\,\mathsf{S}\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) , standing among the best performing anhydrous MOF proton conductors at elevated temperature. The interframe dynamics represents an unique feature of MOFs that can be accessed in the future design of proton conductors.
## Introduction
Structure dynamics is one of key features in biologic systems that allows for accommodation to guest molecules, selective chemical conversions, and concerted ion transportation.1-3 Introduction of such controllable dynamics into artificial solid-state materials is challenging, but it is likely to promise enhanced physical and chemical properties.4-10 Metalorganic frameworks (MOFs), constructed by linking organic molecules with metal joints, provide precise control on their pore size and geometry,11-13 thus they have great potential to exercise structure dynamics and to optimize their properties (Scheme 1 and Scheme S1), such as proton conductivity.14-17 One ideal way to introduce dynamic operations in 0 MOFs is stitching molecular building blocks with mechanically interlocked components into the framework (Scheme S1B).18 Such building blocks usually occupy relatively large space, thus, this approach is demanding on the pore size of the corresponding MOF. Another way to create structure dynamics in MOFs is interpenetration, where two or more sets of identical frames coexist in the same crystal but remain geometrically catenated.19,20 The shift between these sets gives rise to structure dynamics and leads to the opening and closing of the pores, hence precisely controls the behaviour of guest molecules (Scheme S1C).21 Dynamics in neither approach involves the change of coordination geometry at the metal joints, which is different from the common flexible MOFs (Scheme S1A).22,23 The number of MOFs exhibiting interpenetrated structures is large, but most of them are dense, not suited for structure dynamics or interaction with guest molecules. For the rest of them, only a few allow for shift between different sets of frames, but for the ones do have such capability, they usually exhibit excellent properties, such as selective inclusion and gas separation.21,24
