In this study, we revealed that dynamics between frames in an interpenetrated MOF offers excellent accommodation for molecules to achieve high proton conductivity in anhydrous condition at elevated temperature. Specifically, we synthesized a three-dimensional (3D) titanium catecholate framework, MOF-217, with 2-fold interpenetrated srs topology (Fig. 1A), where dynamics between frames was observed upon the inclusion and removal of guest molecules (Fig. 1B). This MOF was composed of a \(C_{3\mathrm{h}}\) -symmetry catecholate linker, TDHT, capable of dihedral angle change at catecholate units (Fig. S1A), therefore inducing both twist and shift between different sets of frames (Scheme 1 and Fig. 1C). The structure dynamics was investigated by single crystal X-ray diffraction (SXRD), where the change in both unit cell and interframe distance were clearly observed. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data showed that the inclusion and removal of imidazole into this MOF resulted in \(21\%\) change in unit cell volume. The dihedral angle between TDHT linker (purple triangle in Fig. 1C) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{6}\) cluster (yellow triangle in
Fig. 1C) at opposite position of the same pore on the same frame was twisted from \(15~^{\circ}\) to \(50~^{\circ}\), while the interframe distance switched between \(9.8\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) and \(3.3\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\). The accommodation of imidazole into pores of MOF-217 led to drastic promotion in proton conductivity by four orders of magnitude in comparison to activated MOF. MOF-217 with \(28\%\) of imidazole by weight, \(28\%\,\mathrm{Im}\) -in-MOF-217, exhibited a proton conductivity of \(1.1~\times\) \(10^{-3}\;\mathrm{S\;cm}^{-1}\) at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and can be operated for 23 hours at \(85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), providing a new approach for the design of molecular based anhydrous proton conductors.
## Results and discussion
Titanium MOFs are known to exhibit excellent chemical stability due to the strong Ti-O bond in their structures.25 Unlike
the large family of stable MOFs constructed by \(Z\mathbf{r}\) clusters,26,27 MOFs composed of Ti clusters are relatively rare.28,34 This is mainly attributed to the fast hydrolysis of titanium precursors in the reaction, disfavouring the crystallization process. Here, the synthesis of MOF-217 was conducted by solvothermal reaction of 2,4,6-tri(3,4-dihydroxyphenyl)-1,3,5-triazine (TDHT) with titanium isopropoxide (TTIP) in Schlenk tube. Precise control of reaction temperature and moisture level was implemented to reach a balance between hydrolysis of Ti precursor and deprotonation of catechol linker. If the reaction temperature dropped below \(150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), insufficient dimethylammonium cations (DMA ions) could be generated for the removal of protons from catechol linkers, resulting in the formation of undesirable amorphous products. Therefore, the growth of MOF-217 was conducted at \(180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 48 hours to yield red octahedral shaped
crystals with suitable size for SXRD analysis. It was worth noting that the use of anhydrous N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) didn't improve the crystallinity of MOF. In contrast, regular analytical pure DMF with trace amount of water was suited to give large single crystals.
The phase purity of MOF-217 was confirmed by the singular nature of the octahedral crystal morphology as shown in optical microscopy image and the crystallinity of MOF-217 was evidenced by its sharp peaks in PXRD pattern (Fig. S6). The atomic structure of MOF-217 was identified by SXRD performed on its single crystal at 100K, revealing a space group of \(P\mathbf{a}{-}3\) (Table S1). SXRD data was also collected at elevated temperature, 200K and 250K (Table S2 and Table S3), where \(P\mathbf{a}{-}3\) space group remained unchanged. In the crystal structure of MOF-217, each Ti ion was coordinated with six oxygen atoms from three TDHT linkers to give a distorted octahedral shape (Fig. S8). The linkage of three-connecting \(\mathrm{TiO}_{6}\) cluster and tritopic catecholate linker resulted in a typical \([3+3]\) srs topology with 2-fold interpenetrated structure (Fig. 1A).35
The design and synthesis of TDHT linker is the key to give twist and sliding dynamics in MOF-217. MOFs based on catecholate linkers have been successfully synthesized, including Ti-MOF.28,36,37 Most of the catecholate linkers in previous works are composed of fused rings and usually have high symmetry with rotational operation axis or mirror planes in between the adjacent -OH groups in the same catechol ring (Fig. S1B). TDHT linkers in this work exhibited C-C single bonds between the centre triazine ring and the catechol ring, with one -OH group at both meta- and para- positions. This allowed for the possibility of rotation along the C-C bond, where the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{6}\) clusters are not on the rotation operation axis along the C-C bond. Unlike rigid catechol linkers in previous work, the rotation along the C-C bond in TDHT led to the change of \(C_{3\mathrm{h}}\) symmetry of TDHT linker to \(C_{3}\) symmetry, thus inducing dynamics into the backbone of MOF-217 (Fig. S1A and Fig. 1B). This symmetry change involved the breaking of hydrogen bonds between nitrogen atom in triazine ring and hydrogen atoms in adjacent catechol rings. The structure dynamics was clearly observed in SXRD studies, where the unit cell parameters of the same crystal changed as the temperature increased, \(\mathbf{a}\!=\!20.9953(3)\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) at 100K, and \(\mathbf{a}\!=\!21.1222(4)\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) at 250K. The change in bond length of C5- C7 is more obvious, 1.481(3) \(\mathring\mathrm{A}\) at 100K, and 1.470(2) \(\mathring\mathrm{A}\) at 250K (Table S4). In addition, the distance between two interpenetrated frames in single crystal data was increased from \(6.8\mathrm{~\AA~}\) at 100K to \(7.0\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\) at 250K, indicating the expansion of the net and the shift between frames (Fig. S9 and Table S5).
The formation of Ti-O bond was confirmed by the presence of characteristic Ti-O stretching peaks \(^{38}\ 633\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), in Fourier transfer infrared spectrum (FTIR) of the activated MOF-217 (Fig. S10). The complete deprotonation of TDHT linker was evidenced by the disappearance of \(\mathrm{C-H}\) stretching peaks in MOF-217 (Fig. S11), as all of the -OH groups in TDHT linker were coordinated with Ti cations. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis of activated sample revealed the coexistence of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\) in MOF backbone, where two peaks were unambiguously observed in Ti 2p profile (Fig. S13). This
was consistent with the presence of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) signal, \(\mathrm{g}\,{=}\,\sqrt{1.924}\) in the electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) Spectrums of the same sample (Fig. S14). Surprisingly, additional EPR signal with the g value of 2.005 was observed, which can be assigned to semiquinone radicals.39 It was known that catechol can be partially charged to form semiquinone, which explained the presence of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) in the MOF structure.40 This was also reflected in the irregular bond lengths of C-O and C-C adjacent to Ti1 in single crystal structure of MOF-217 at different temperature (Table S4), a phenomenon also observed in previous report of a cobalt-based catecholate MOF.40 MOF-217 exhibited thermal stability up to \(300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in air as revealed by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) (Fig. S15). Proton nuclear magnetic resonance \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) -NMR) study of the digested MOF sample in DCl/DMSO- \(d_{6}\) solution determined the presence of compensatory negative charges, DMA cations, in the framework (Fig. S16). Quantified analysis showed the ratio of DMA cations to TDHT linkers is 1.58:1. This combined with the elemental analysis (EA) (Table S6), giving a formula of \(\mathrm{[Ti(TDHT)}\) \((\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{1.58}(\mathrm{DMF})_{0.15}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{0.5}(\mathrm{NBuBr})_{0.15}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2.25}]\) for MOF-217. PXRD was also performed on MOF-217 crystals after immersion in water, a HCl aqueous solution with \(\mathrm{pH}=4\) or a NaOH aqueous solution with \(\mathrm{pH}=11\) for 50 hours, where the PXRD patterns were essentially the same to that of the original MOF, indicating the high chemical stability of MOF-217 (Fig. S17).
The structure dynamics of MOF-217 is favourable to its proton conductivity. MOFs have been successfully applied as efficient proton conductors, taking the advantage for the inclusion of small molecules into porous crystals.41-46 Different from MOF-based proton conductors capable of backbone distortion (Scheme 1A),14-17 MOF-217 in this study exhibited a different type of structure dynamics, twist and sliding between the interpenetrated frames (Scheme 1D). We found that this new type of dynamics offered excellent accommodation to molecules, here imidazole, a proton carrier known to promote the proton conductivity of solid-state porous materials when be introduced into their pores.47-50 The inclusion of imidazole into MOF-217 led to drastic boost in proton conductivity, from \(1.9\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) to \(1.1\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Prior to the detailed discussion on their proton conductivities, we studied the structure dynamics induced by the pore filling of imidazole molecules. Imidazole content in MOF-217 can be precisely tuned from \(16\%\) to \(32\%\) by weight (1.8 imidazole for 1 Ti cation to 4.5 imidazole for 1 Ti cation), where the accurate imidazole content in each sample was simultaneously quantified by TGA and EA (Fig. S18 and Table S8). PXRD studies were performed on MOF-217 with different imidazole contents, using the activated MOF sample as control (Fig. S20). MOF-217 filled with \(16\%\) of imidazole by weight exhibited a PXRD pattern similar to that of the activated MOF. As the imidazole content increased to \(18\%\) by weight, however, systematic variation in both peak positions and intensities were observed, indicating the change of unit cell parameters as a result of structure dynamics of MOF-217. These systematic changes continued to take place until the imidazole content increased to \(28\%\) by weight (3.6 imidazole for 1 Ti cation), where the pores
were saturated. Further increase of imidazole content resulted in the deposition of imidazole crystals outside MOF pores (Fig. S21), which can be reflected in the presence of extra peaks in PXRD patterns (Fig. S20). In order to unveil the quantitative change in unit cell parameters, Pawley refinements were performed on small-angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) data of these samples (Fig. 2A to 2C), where low convergence residuals were
achieved in these refinements for all these samples. \(21\%\) change in unit cell volume was observed between activated sample and \(28\%\) Im-in-MOF-217 (Table S9). PXRD data was also collected using synchrotron X-ray source (Fig. 2D to 2F and Fig. S22), which allowed for Rietveld refinement. The refinement result gave a satisfactory residual, revealing the twist within the same frame along [111] axis and the sliding between different frames
upon the filling of imidazole into pores (Fig. 2G to 2I). Specifically, the angle between opposite triangular counterparts (purple and yellow triangles in Fig. 1C), representing the tri-topic linker and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{6}\) cluster, switched from \(15\,^{\circ}\) to \(50\,^{\circ}\), while the interframe distance tuned from \(9.8\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) to \(3.3\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\). This clearly demonstrated the capability of drastic structure dynamics in this double interpenetrated MOF.
Variable temperature in situ PXRD (VT-PXRD) was investigated on the as-synthesized MOF-217 with temperature range from \(25\mathrm{{^\circC}}\) to \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), as shown in Fig. S24. Systematic changes in both peak positions and intensities were observed, indicating dynamics of the structure upon temperature variation. After the VT-PXRD test, this sample was solvated by immersion in DMF. The sharp peaks in the PXRD pattern and the excellent match to that of the as-synthesized sample further confirmed the thermal stability of MOF-217. In addition, immersion of activated MOF-217 back into the solvents led to the regeneration of its crystallinity (Fig. S25). Also, we synthesized a previously reported Ti-MOF, Ti-CAT-5, with similar topology but different symmetry in the organic linker (Fig. S13). Imidazole filling into its pores in similar amount to that in MOF-217 was performed (Fig. S19 and Table S8). However, neither the peak position nor the peak intensity was varied upon the inclusion of imidazole in Ti-CAT-5 (Fig. S23), which is distinctively different from that observed in MOF-217. This again illustrated the importance of structure dynamics for the appropriate accommodation of imidazole molecules.
The proton conductivities of activated MOF-217 and the ones with imidazole filling in pores were tested by alternating current (ac) impedance spectroscopy, using Ti-CAT-5 counterparts as control. Nyquist plots from impedance test of these samples were collected at elevated temperature \((30{-}100^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) under a nitrogen atmosphere. Based on impedance experiment, the proton conductivity of activated MOF-217 was calculated, e.g. \(1.9\times10^{-7}\;\mathrm{S\;cm}^{-1}\) at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which was similar to that of Ti-CAT-5, \(3.8\times10^{-8}\;\mathrm{S\;cm}^{-1}\) at the same temperature (Fig. S30 and Fig. S31). Analysis of the impedance at various temperature revealed Arrhenius plot of each sample that gave their corresponding activation energies. Based on the activation energy, there are two different mechanisms for proton conduction in general: (1) vehicle mechanism, transferring protons by the diffusion of protonated carriers with activation energy more than \(0.4\ \mathrm{\eV}\), and (2) Grothuss mechanism, transferring protons between H-donor and acceptor with activation energy less than \(0.4\;\mathrm{eV}\). Activation energies derived from these slopes in Arrhenius plots (Fig. 3B) were \(0.89~\mathrm{eV}\) for MOF-217 and \(0.24\ \mathrm{\eV}\) for Ti-CAT-5, respectively, both in activated form. In this regard, the proton conduction for MOF217 is vehicle mechanism, distinctively different from that of Ti-CAT-5. Moreover, protons on DMA cations are hopping along with hydrogen bond network in Ti-CAT-5, while DMA cations are diffusing through framework of MOF-217 for proton conduction. High temperature may destroy the hydrogen bond network in Ti-CAT-5, thus resulting in the decrease in the mobility of protons and proton conductivity. In contrast, higher temperature will favour the structure dynamics of MOF-217,
which provides higher degree of freedom for the travelling of DMA cations, therefore increasing the mobility of proton carriers.
MOF-217 with \(16\%\) to \(28\%\) of imidazole by weight into the pores exhibited dramatically improved proton conductivity, \(1.3\times10^{-5}\) and \(1.1\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\;cm}^{-1}\) at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S32, Fig. S33 and Fig. 3A). The activation energies of these samples were also derived using the data up to \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 3B), at which stable operation was achieved. As the imidazole content increased, the activation energy decreased gradually. Molecular dynamic calculations were carried out to assess the radial distribution functions (RDF) for distance between H-acceptors and H-donors (Fig. S37 to Fig. S39), and the number of H-bonds in MOF-217 (Fig. S40) with different imidazole contents. The results revealed the formation of a larger portion of H-bond between N atom in the imidazole and O atom in MOF structure, as imidazole was introduced. Therefore, a mixed mechanism of both vehicle and Grothuss was likely to present, which is consistent with the decreased activation energies. The increase in the imidazole content in MOF pores led to a more pronounced contribution from the interaction between proton carrier and MOF, hence the further promotion in proton conductivity.
The proton conductivity of a material is dictated by both the concentration and mobility of proton carriers within this material.47 Comparing between MOF-217 and Ti-CAT-5 with identical imidazole content (28% by weight), drastically higher proton conductivity was observed in MOF-217, \(1.1 \times 10^{-3}\) S \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 3A and Fig. S34). This value is four orders of magnitude higher than that of activated MOF-217, one magnitude higher than that of \(28\%\) Im-in-Ti-CAT-5 under identical condition, and is among the best performing MOF materials under similar condition (Table S10 and Table S11). The activation energy of \(28\%\) Im-in-MOF-217 was lower than that of \(28\%\) Im-in-Ti-CAT-5 (Fig. 3B), indicating more involvement of MOF frames in proton conduction. This is likely due to the better contact between MOF and imidazole in the pores, another advantage of flexibility between interpenetrated sets. The recycle test was performed on \(28\%\) Im-in-MOF-217 to evaluate the dynamics and stability of material. Continuous test at \(85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) revealed negligible decrease in proton conductivity within 23 hours (Fig. S35). It was worth noting that the crystallinity of MOF-217 was fully preserved throughout the entire impedance test, as evidenced by its unaltered PXRD pattern (Fig. S36).
## Conclusions
We synthesized a Ti-MOF, MOF-217, using a catecholate linker capable of switching between \(C_{3\mathrm{h}}\) and \(C_{3}\) symmetry. This MOF exhibited a double interpenetrated structure, where twist and sliding dynamics was observed upon temperature change and the inclusion of molecules into its pores. Structure dynamics in imidazole within MOF-217 offered extra freedom for proton carrier, leading to drastic promotion in its proton conductivity by four orders of magnitude. This twist and sliding dynamics introduced into MOFs represents a new way for the design of molecular based anhydrous proton conductors.
## Experimental
## Materials and methods
N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), methanol, petroleum ether (PE), dichloromethane \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\right)\), chloroform \(\left(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\right)\), acetonitrile (MeCN), ammonium hydroxide, trifluoromethanesulfonic acid \(\left(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\right)\), imidazole, and sodium sulfate \(\left(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\right)\) were obtained from China National Medicines Corporation LTD. N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, anhydrous, \(98\%\) ) and methanol (anhydrous, \(98\%\) ) were purchased from J&K Chemical Co.. Boron tribromide \(\left(\mathrm{BBr}_{3}, 1.0\right.\) M in \(\left.\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\right)\) was purchased from Aladdin. Titanium (IV) isopropoxide (TTIP), tetrabutylammonium bromide \(\left(\mathrm{NBu}_{4}\mathrm{Br}\right)\), amylamine and 3,4-dimethoxybenzonitrile were purchased from Sigma Aldrich Chemical Company. 2,3,6,7,10,11-Hexahydroxytriphenylene (HHTP) was obtained from TCI. Chloroform and dichloromethane were dried by freshly activated molecular sieves 4A prior to use. Other chemicals were used without further purifications. All manipulations of air and
moisture sensitive materials were conducted under \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) nitrogen atmosphere in a glovebox or on Schlenk line.10,10,1039/C9SC06500H
## Synthesis and activation of MOF-217
TDHT (12 mg, 0.030 mmol) was dissolved in \(400~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) DMF and frozen with a bath of liquid nitrogen in Schlenk tube. \(\mathrm{NBu}_{4}\mathrm{Br}\) (10 mg, 0.031 mmol) was dissolved in \(400~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) DMF and then 100 \(\upmu\mathrm{l}\) methanol with \(4~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) amylamine as modulator was added. The solution of titanium isopropoxide \(\left[\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{i}-\mathrm{OPr})_{4}, 7.5~\upmu\mathrm{l}, 0.025\right.\) mmol] in \(200~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) DMF was injected quickly into this frozen system. These reactants were immediately sealed and vacuumed through a freeze-pump way. Reaction was naturally turned to ambient temperature, stirring for 30 minutes and heating at \(180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 48 hours, then red octahedral shaped crystals were yield. As-synthesized sample was refluxed by DMF for 1 day and methanol for another 1 day. Next, MOF sample was transferred to supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) drier and washed 5 times with liquid \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (liquid \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was refreshed every 15 minutes) for removing residual solvents. During this process, the chamber was heated up to \(38^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and held on the supercritical condition (typically 1300 psi) for 45 minutes, then \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was slowly bled from the chamber for nearly 6 hours. Finally, activated MOF-217 was obtained.
## Synthesis and activation of Ti-CAT-5
Ti-CAT-5 was synthesized by an improved procedure based on reference.28 Titanium isopropoxide \(\left[\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{i}-\mathrm{OPr})_{4}, 9~\upmu\mathrm{l}, 0.03\right.\) mmol], HHTP (10 mg, 0.030 mmol), \(\mathrm{NBu}_{4}\mathrm{Br}\) (10 mg, 0.031 mmol) were dissolved in \(1~\mathrm{ml}\) anhydrous DMF and \(30~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) anhydrous methanol, with \(4~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) amylamine as modulator. Then the mixture was transferred into an \(8~\mathrm{mm}\) dia glass tube which was quickly frozen and vacuumed through a freeze-pump way. The tube was put into oven and stood for 48 hours at \(180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). After reaction was cool down to room temperature, brown powder was collected by centrifugation at \(11000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for 5 minutes. As-synthesized sample was washed by DMF for 5 times and MeCN for 9 times, then vacuumed at room temperature for 24 hours to get activated sample. Characterizations as PXRD, IR, XPS, TGA, EA and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) absorption were performed to identify the successful synthesis of Ti-CAT-5 (Fig. S7, Fig. S12, Fig. S13, Fig. S15, Table S8, Fig. S26, Fig. S27 and Fig. S28).
## Imidazole loading and ac impedance experiment
Imidazole was grinded with activated MOF-217 and sealed in a tube, then stood at \(120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under reduced pressure overnight. Also, imidazole was embedded into Ti-CAT-5 in the same way as proton conduction comparison. Powdery samples were pressed into a pellet with \(12~\mathrm{mm}\) dia and \(\sim0.4~\mathrm{mm}\) thickness. Each pellet was sandwiched by two stainless electrodes, a spring for good contact, and sealed into a button cell. Considering the humidity in air, this process was manipulated in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) glovebox. Both sides of the cell were attached by silver wires, then put into a homemade chamber for temperature control. Alternating current (ac) impedance analysis was measured on a biologic VPM-3 electrochemical workstation with \(1~\mathrm{Hz}-10~\mathrm{MHz}\) frequency range and \(10~\mathrm{mV}\) input voltage amplitude at different temperature \((30-100^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). Zsimpwin software was used to simulate equivalent circuit (Fig. S29) to match the Nyquist plots
and obtain resistance values. Finally, resistance values were input in equation \(\scriptstyle\left(\sigma=\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{RS}\right)\) to get conductivity values.
